the protests against police brutality  dominated much of the news this week  rioting looting and violence grabbed  many of the headlines but out of the  smoldering anger some moments of peace  and understanding last Saturday after  protesters and police faced off  nationwide chief Andrew Mills of the  Santa Cruz California Police Department  took a different approach and that  determined in my mind that I wanted to  take a knee with the community to show  my support for our community that there  can be a peaceful way to do this without  having right here and we as a community  can do better that we as the police  department must be better in make sure  that we're not policing from a  standpoint of bias after chief Mills  statement other law enforcement officers  across the country followed when the  protesters asked me to take a knee it  was it was an easy thought it was there  was nothing hard for me to think about I  want people to know that I did it for  peace I did it for unity and I did it  for healing  there were no barriers between police  and protesters in Camden New Jersey with  officers marching in lockstep with  protesters  in Nashville where earlier in the week  City Hall have been set ablaze  demonstrators convinced National  Guardsmen to lay down their riot shields  I think the engagement that we saw in  Nashville last night gives us a glimpse  of what can be and I'm very thankful for  that  other demonstrators like Pittsburgh  protest organizer Alexander Cash have  also reached out  all the stuff that they did downtown is  really tragic I don't I want to show  that we can be peaceful what do you want  to tell your children about this moment  still the delicate tension remains as  the nation waits to see whether voices  of reason endure  we can't keep repeating our faults and  our errors because we lost too many  lives doing that
 

